{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem explicitly requires drawing on published definitions to establish the precise distinction between idempotent and safe. SRC_001's definitions, derivation, and formalization_refs arrays are all empty, and it proposes no research fetches. Its offered distinction (safety = identifiable retries at the write layer, idempotency = deduplication effect at the replay layer) is an architectural role assignment, not the precise conceptual distinction the problem asks for. The standard published distinction is mathematical: idempotency means f∘f = f (repeating the operation yields the same effect as performing it once), whereas safety means the operation cannot drive the system into a state that violates invariants. SRC_001 never articulates this, so it fails to meet the problem's stated requirement to ground the distinction in published definitions.","target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's central claim rests on an unstated assumption that 'deterministic replay' means byte-for-byte log identity. The problem asks about 'deterministic replay in an append-only reasoning system,' which in event-sourcing terminology means that replaying the same logical event sequence reproduces the same derived state — not that the raw log bytes are identical. SRC_002's own counterconditions concede the point: 'If deterministic replay is defined at the semantic level (after dedup) rather than the byte level, then safety suffices and this claim is too strong.' Because the target itself acknowledges that the alternative interpretation collapses its argument, its conclusion that writers must be idempotent is conditional on a reading the problem does not mandate. The target also provides no published definitions (empty definitions, derivation, formalization_refs), failing the problem's explicit requirement to draw on them.","target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}